WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.760
Two weeks ago Charlottesville erupted into
chaos.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.830
Violence met the streets as a groups of white
supremacists, neo-Nazis, and far right demonstrators

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:21.450
clashed with counter-protesters.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.820
Some have criticized and blamed the violence
on the police response.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.570
“The police had every opportunity.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.230
They’re getting overtime pay, they’re
getting hazard pay, they’ve got tens of

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:36.070
thousands of dollars of equipment per officer
and they came out here and guarded empty space

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.390
the entire day.”

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.720
But to better understand why the demonstration
ended up the way it did, you have to understand

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.949
how riot control works.

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:51.530
The fundamental problem with riot control
is that there are almost never more riot police

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.269
than rioters so the police need to artificially
give themselves an advantage.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:57.809
Part of it is psychological.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.960
They make it so rioters think that the police
could defeat them.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.729
The uniforms riot police wear are intentionally
dehumanizing.

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:09.690
Clad in protective gear head-to-toe, you can
hardly tell one person from the next.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.960
This creates a psychological barrier between
the police and the rioters.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.280
Studies have show that these uniforms make
both protesters and the police themselves

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.390
feel like the police officers are more powerful
than they are.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:26.840
It has also been proven that the way riot
police act increases their perceived power.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.710
The officers almost always march and act in
unison.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:35.429
When they act as a collective, just like the
protestors, they personally feel as if they

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:39.380
have the power of the collective and the crowd
does too.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.600
The most basic riot police formation looks
like this.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.530
With the goal of moving the crowd to another
location, this front echelon slowly advances,

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.280
pushing rioters forward with their shields
and batons.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:55.030
They’re followed by a team leader who organizes
the whole group, most likely this officer

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.950
in this Charlottesville footage.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.869
Right with the team leader are a few gas officers.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:01.189
You can see one here.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:05.499
They have tear gas or pepper spray to deploy
over the front echelon towards any demonstrators

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:07.080
that put up a fight.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.460
Following them is a group of arrest officers.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.190
Should the front group encounter any particularly
difficult rioters, they can open up, let the

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.870
demonstrator though, then the arresting officers
can take the troublemaker into custody.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:23.150
Finishing is the rear echelon which protects
the group and can sub out with the front echelon

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.730
when they tire.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:30.099
Violence happens at rallies because of three
facts: crowds are anonymous, anonymity breeds

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:33.420
violence, and violence lowers consequences.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.430
Crowds are deindividualizing.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.450
When you’re just one of a number, you don’t
think or act as much like an individual person.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.800
A group of people all with a common goal and
a common way of thinking breeds a collective

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.049
conscience.

00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:51.659
In a crowd, people don’t think about consequences
the same way they do when they’re on their

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:52.659
own.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:54.280
There’s a sort of contagion of feeling.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.769
Just like a sports game or a nightclub, people
act differently than they normally might because

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:00.180
other people do too.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.010
There’s a sort of wordless peer-pressure.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.560
Individuals subconsciously escalate their
violence to match that of the leaders.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.840
People act almost by instinct in crowds and
the individual breaks down and becomes a part

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.319
of the collective.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.720
When there’s widespread violence, individuals
are punished less.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:22.909
There’s a lower sense of legal culpability
since police often make far fewer arrests

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:23.910
than normal.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.920
In a riot, authorities can’t arrest everyone.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.890
They target the leaders, the most violent
protestors.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:31.060
It’s like running from a bear.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.090
You don’t have to be faster than the bear,
you just have to be faster than the slowest

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.160
person.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.560
Dozens or perhaps hundreds of people committed
violent acts in Charlottesville that normally

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.180
would have resulted in arrests, but out of
them, only eight were arrested.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.900
Police have to be very careful with the directionality
of the protest.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.830
The police’s goal is to stop or slow the
riot while widespread arrests will often further

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.560
ignite the violence.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.001
In Charlottesville, for example, if the police
had intervened too strongly, the direction

00:04:01.001 --> 00:04:05.120
could have turned and members of both groups
could have directed their violence towards

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.319
the police.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:11.260
Police never want to be perceived as unjust,
in a riot scenario that is dangerous, so they

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:16.329
have to play a careful balancing act between
too little and too much response.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.769
So what went wrong in Charlottesville?

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.670
Why did it end up so violent and could the
police have prevented it?

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:27.250
The “Unite the Right” rally was initially
slated to begin at noon but by 9 am there

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.500
were already hundreds of demonstrators from
both sides.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.760
The police were not ready that early.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.710
They weren’t in their riot uniforms and
they didn’t have significant numbers.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.300
The initial plan was to physically separate
the two groups on separate sides of Emancipation

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.830
park in downtown Charlottesville.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:48.160
There were barriers set up, but nobody seemed
to anticipate the number of people that showed

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.160
up.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.810
The “Unite the Right” rally was in fact
a permitted assembly.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.700
That group’s presence at Emancipation park
was fully legal, but the city did not want

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:57.860
them there specifically.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:02.750
The city government tried to block the demonstration
permit unless the group agreed to hold their

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:08.580
rally at nearby McIntire park, a much larger
and more open park nearby, but the rally’s

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:13.380
organizers successfully contested this move
in court on first amendment grounds and was

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.350
allowed to hold their rally right there in
downtown Charlottesville.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:21.950
There simply was not enough space for the
number of people who showed up, but the fundamental

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.710
issue in Charlottesville was numbers.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.590
Charlottesville is a small town, fewer than
50,000 people live there, and their police

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:31.770
force is correspondently small.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:38.170
While there were thousands of protestors,
the police force had fewer than 130 officers.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.780
At the same time, the officers working in
Charlottesville were hardly experienced with

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:43.780
riots.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.790
It’s not a big city with frequent protests—this
might have been the first time many officers

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.620
used their riot gear in the field.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.830
They could not afford for the violence to
turn towards them.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.940
While the chief of police hasn’t spoke much
about his tactics, experts have said that

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.400
the lack of initial intervention was likely
a conscious choice to assure that the violence

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:06.430
stayed between protestors and protestors,
not between protestors and police.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:12.150
Virginia is an open-carry state and many demonstrators
carried assault weapons so escalated aggression

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.800
could have turned the riot even deadlier.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.990
At around 11:40 am, the Virginia State Police
declared the protest an unlawful assembly

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.410
meaning it was then illegal to participate.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.440
They subsequently began the process of breaking
it up carefully.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.950
Now, how you break up a protest is very important.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:30.730
Doing it wrong can turn deadly.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.620
Their first priority was clearing Emancipation
Park.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.030
This was both the symbolic and physical center
of the protest.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.806
They made it know that the assembly was now
unlawful, “This gathering has been declared

00:06:38.806 --> 00:06:49.530
to be an unlawful assembly,” then began
to slowly work their way outwards in a uniform

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.570
fashion pushing back anyone who put up a fight.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:56.100
They strategically removed the violence leaders
from the public area.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.540
Here you can see the man in red pulled through
the front echelon of officers.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:03.670
(12:10) He was likely brought into custody
by the arrest officers behind the front echelon

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.780
since he was one of the main escalators.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.870
There were other aggressors escalating the
situation like this man with the flag, but

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.410
the police didn’t risk breaking rank to
take him into custody.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:17.140
Doing so would expose officers to the violence
of the main crowd.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.570
Tear gas was used to get the final people
out of the park.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:24.280
Part of the reason tear gas is so effective
is because the discomfort it brings makes

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.890
people stop their collective action to worry
about themselves.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:32.210
It takes them out of the mass and has them
concentrate on the individual.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:36.960
The park was eventually cleared but the riot
largely continued on the surrounding streets.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.150
What was important was that they police did
not try to contain the demonstrators.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.620
They did try to contain the violence, but
not the people.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:49.590
Whenever riot police intervenes, they always
want to leave an escape route for the demonstrators

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.090
who decide that they’ve had enough and want
to leave.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:58.810
A big reason why riot police look and act
so intimidatingly is to get rioters to leave.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:03.730
This is, in fact, exactly how you stop a riot—by
getting people to leave.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:08.630
In Charlottesville, after the clearing of
Emancipation Park, many of the alt-right protestors

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:14.280
moved to a secondary location while the counter-protestors
starting marching in the surrounding streets.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:18.920
After many hours of violence, the groups did,
to an extent, naturally separate from each

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:24.070
other given the additional space and pressure
of the illegality of the protest.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:28.020
The police had set up a plan to physically
separate the opposing groups from the start

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.200
but that was an idealistic plan.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:35.580
It was unrealistic to think that passionate,
aggressive protestors would self-select into

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:36.860
their proper areas.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:41.329
It’s hard to know exactly if the police
could have done a better job at preventing

00:08:41.329 --> 00:08:42.329
violence.

00:08:42.329 --> 00:08:46.560
From analysis its clear that they were overwhelmingly
cautious in their techniques which some may

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.589
consider merited given their inexperience
with riots and disadvantage in numbers.

00:08:51.589 --> 00:08:55.990
The techniques riot police use are designed
to give them an advantage where they don’t

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:56.990
have one.

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:01.959
They’re tasked with preventing damage, injuries,
and death but they do have to play a careful

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:06.889
game of balance to make sure that violence
isn’t immediately directed towards them.

00:09:06.889 --> 00:09:12.080
If they’re overwhelmed with directed violence
they can’t effectively prevent damaging

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.080
violence.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.420
The police are in a position where they’re
criticized when they have too little response

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:18.610
and criticized when they have too much.

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:23.269
Everyone’s opinion on the proper level of
response differs so it’s almost impossible

00:09:23.269 --> 00:09:26.579
for an assembly to occur without criticism
of the police.

00:09:26.579 --> 00:09:31.389
Charlottesville was an unfortunate situation
where thousands of people all came into a

00:09:31.389 --> 00:09:36.339
tiny town to hold one of the most violent
and passionate protests of this decade.

00:09:36.339 --> 00:09:41.220
The best analysis of the police’s response
may be in the outcome: there where no directly

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.190
preventable deaths and damage to the city
was minimal.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.220
The United States and many other countries
around the world are centered on the idea

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:53.810
of free speech, so these police officers have
the unenviable job of deciding where the demonstration

00:09:53.810 --> 00:10:00.680
of this unalienable right stops and where
dangerous, violent hooliganism begins.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.670
On a lighter note, I’ll be launching a brand
new channel this Thursday, August 31st.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:11.379
I don’t want to reveal too much, but it’s
essentially the continuation of the old TWL

00:10:11.379 --> 00:10:12.379
series.

00:10:12.379 --> 00:10:16.319
All you should do now is subscribe here to
get the first video right when it comes out.

